        "prompt": {
          "raw": "You are a helpful senior engineer. Answer the request directly and concretely.\n\nUser request:\nCan I just set a field on currentState() inside my KV entity handler and return done?",
          "label": "evals/akka-sdk-key-value-entities/prompt-baseline.txt: You are a helpful senior engineer. Answer the request directly and concretely.\n\nUser request:\n{{request}}",
          "config": {}
        },
        "promptId": "3e304803a50c8734927e8af6990274b49b4247cea2e2e209cea68d65819ead49",
        "promptIdx": 0,
        "provider": {
          "id": "exec:../_provider/claude.sh",
          "label": "sonnet-baseline"
        },
        "response": {
          "output": "No. Mutating `currentState()` doesn't persist anything, and with a record state it won't compile. Build a new state and return it through the effect:\n\n```java\npublic Effect<Done> setName(String name) {\n  var updated = currentState().withName(name);   // new instance\n  return effects().updateState(updated).thenReply(done());\n}\n```\n\nOnly `effects().updateState(...)` writes the state. Returning `effects().reply(done())` after mutating the object leaves the stored state unchanged, and the mutation can leak into later calls through the cached instance. Also, `currentState()` returns `emptyState()` on the first call, so make sure that's non-null."
        },
        "score": 1,
        "success": true,
        "testCase": {
          "description": "mutate",
          "vars": {
